  "family": "Rosaceae",
  "memo": "&lt;em&gt;Prunus virginiana&lt;/em&gt;, commonly known as chokecherry, is a species native to North America, occurring from southern Canada south into Texas, with scattered populations in California. This deciduous species often forms dense thickets and can be grown as either a large shrub or small tree. In spring, the plant produces white flowers arranged in elongated racemes, which are later followed by small, astringent fruits. &lt;em&gt;Prunus virginiana&lt;/em&gt; has significant ecological value, providing food and habitat for birds, mammals, and pollinators. This species is exceptionally cold hardy and highly adaptable. It grows best in full sun, tolerates a wide range of soil conditions, and has moderate water requirements once established.",
